  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Sunday)\nIssued at 1037 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will build in from the west Monday and more \nprominently heading into mid week. Surface temperatures will quickly \nrespond, with low to mid 90s Monday, and upper 90s to low 100s for \nthe remainder of the week. As southerly flow returns, moisture \nlevels will begin to rise again. The combination of heat and \nhumidity will likely result in Heat Advisory potential by mid \nweek for some areas. Typically, the Tulsa area and Arkansas River \nValley are most susceptible to reaching heat criteria in \nborderline situations.\n\nNo rain is expected the next few days. By late Wednesday, and more \nlikely on Thursday or Friday, rain chances will begin to increase\nslightly. This will occur as a distant upper level trough sends a\nsurface cold front into the warm and moistening airmass. A weak \nshortwave or two around the periphery of the upper level trough \ncould kick off a few rounds of showers and storms. Overall, \nensemble guidance does not look overly excited, but a few storms \nwith brief heavy rain and gusty winds seem reasonable. Any storm \nactivity will likely focus more to the northeast part of the\nforecast area, particularly over the higher terrain. Otherwise, \nbelow normal precipitation trends appear primed to continue \nthrough mid August.",
